WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.291
External numbers should not be trusted
just because they parse.

2
00:00:03.371 --> 00:00:05.955
Numeric input can come from files, network
messages, APIs,

3
00:00:06.035 --> 00:00:09.653
command-line arguments, environment
variables, database rows, configuration,

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:10.819
or serialized payloads.

5
00:00:10.899 --> 00:00:14.310
A successful parse only says the text or
bytes could be interpreted as a number.

6
00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.734
Validation is a separate step.

7
00:00:15.814 --> 00:00:18.812
A parsed value may be negative when only
positive values are allowed,

8
00:00:18.892 --> 00:00:21.011
too large for a limit, measured in the
wrong unit,

9
00:00:21.091 --> 00:00:23.675
inconsistent with another field, outside a
business rule,

10
00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:26.133
or inappropriate for the current tenant or
operation.

11
00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:28.745
The application must decide whether the
value is allowed.

12
00:00:28.825 --> 00:00:31.048
Defaults and missing values need explicit
policy.

13
00:00:31.128 --> 00:00:34.074
A missing timeout might mean use a safe
default, reject the request,

14
00:00:34.154 --> 00:00:35.653
or inherit a configuration value.

15
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:38.110
A missing limit might be safer than an
unbounded limit,

16
00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.947
but that behavior should be deliberate.

17
00:00:40.027 --> 00:00:43.594
Silent defaults can become security
surprises when callers misunderstand them.

18
00:00:43.674 --> 00:00:45.690
Related fields should be validated
together.

19
00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.922
A count may parse successfully while a
payload length disagrees with it.

20
00:00:49.002 --> 00:00:52.052
A start offset and length may each be
valid alone but invalid as a range.

21
00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:55.440
A rate limit and time window may combine
into an unreasonable amount of work.

22
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.776
Defensive validation checks relationships,
not only individual fields.

23
00:00:58.856 --> 00:01:01.750
A strong Rust pattern is to parse into a
temporary representation,

24
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.621
validate range and meaning, and then
convert into a domain type.

25
00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:08.319
After that conversion, downstream code can
rely on the type's invariant instead of

26
00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:10.260
repeating raw numeric checks everywhere.
